WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.670
(placid music)
- [Jaden] On the surface,

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:04.520
it looks like Apple is
having a pretty good year.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.480
After all, it did become the world's

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.560
most valuable publicly traded company.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.770
But behind the scenes,
it's longstanding disputes

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:16.380
with app developers are
bubbling over into public view.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.700
- Apple is facing backlash

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.690
over the way it runs the App Store

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:25.150
at a time when the App Store
is a key part of its business.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:27.690
- [Jaden] Queue the
"Fortnite" gaming controversy

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:30.280
(placid music)

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.070
Over the summer, Epic
Games publicly rebelled

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.610
against the App Store's 30% fees

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:39.220
and urged players to join
its fight against Apple.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:43.080
- Ladies and gentlemen, Apple
just banned "Fortnite", what?

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.230
- It's going down,
Apple versus "Fortnite".

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.990
- They're probably the one
company out there right now

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:52.730
that is so beloved by the public

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:57.200
that they'll be able to at
least put up the best fight.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.190
- [Jaden] It's not just Epic Games.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:03.010
Spotify, Airbnb, Tinder,
Facebook and Netflix

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:06.880
have all had disputes with
Apple over App Store rules,

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.410
all while lawmakers are scrutinizing Apple

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.720
and other tech companies for
potential antitrust violations.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.470
- What's to stop Apple

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:18.090
from increasing it's commission to 50%?

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.930
- We, sir, we have never
increased commissions

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:25.460
in the store since the first
day it operated in 2008.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.420
- [Jaden] So, what are
app makers asking for

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.663
and is Apple going to listen?

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.530
First, it's important to understand

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.380
how crucial the App Store has
become to Apple's business.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.900
In recent years, Apple's
been shifting its focus

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.390
from selling more devices like iPhones

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.930
to selling more software and services.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.070
Think Apple Music, Apple Care,

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.780
and of course the App Store.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.960
- Apple One gives you and your family

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.810
all the services you
love in one simple plan,

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.160
- [Jaden] In 2020, its services business,

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.980
which includes the App Store,

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.950
helped the company set a new sales record

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.510
in the June quarter.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:04.810
Apple doesn't break out
its App Store revenue,

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:09.310
but analysts estimate it
brought in roughly $17.5 billion

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.820
for fiscal year 2020.
(placid music)

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.980
Some apps and services
are particularly important

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:18.160
for the App Store
ecosystem, like "Fortnite".

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.900
The game has more than a
hundred million registered users

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.460
on Apple devices.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:24.520
That's produced more
than a billion dollars

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.420
in global App Store spending
since its launch in 2018.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.540
But in August, "Fortnite" was kicked

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.430
off the App Store altogether.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.970
That's because the games
creator, Epic Games,

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.750
set up a system to get
money directly from players

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.390
without paying Apple its cut.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.180
Apple takes a 30%
commission on sales of apps

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.950
and in app purchases.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.730
So, if an app costs 4.99 upfront,

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.270
Apple collects a dollar 50,

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.840
while the app creator gets the rest,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.860
same goes for if you use an
app to buy digital goods,

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.020
like virtual weapons or
sign up for a subscription

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.680
to a monthly service,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.910
but the fee doesn't apply to
physical goods and services,

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:06.080
like clothing purchases or home rentals.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.960
- What Epic Games did was it
tried to skirt that commission

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.870
by adding its own payment
system to the "Fortnite" app

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.460
on iPhones and iPads.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:16.990
That goes against Apple's rules.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:20.500
Apple pulled "Fortnite" from
the App Store, Epic sued Apple,

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.370
and then Apple filed a counter suit.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.330
- [Jaden] Google pulled
"Fortnite" from Google Play

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.300
in August for the same reason.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.460
And since the two
companies banned the app,

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.800
their monthly revenue from
the game has taken a big hit.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.970
- From a developer perspective,
it definitely feels

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:38.450
like we're forced to pay
30% right off the top.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.610
- [Jaden] Austin Benson
developed an app for Apple

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:44.400
and Android called Hang that
helped organize meetups.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.470
And he's currently a CTO
at a small tech startup.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:49.800
- It literally feels like a tax

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.310
and those dollars don't really go

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:54.320
towards a government to
pay roads and bridges.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.530
They go to pay two of the biggest

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:59.480
and arguably most powerful
corporations in the world.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:00.770
- [Jaden] In late September,

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:04.950
Spotify, the owner of Tinder
and Epic Games teamed up

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.570
to pressure Apple and
other App Store operators

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:09.760
to change their rules.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.730
Apple and Google have defended
their business practices,

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:14.660
saying the commission fees are necessary

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.100
because of the services
the stores provide,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.031
including security and
safeguarding user privacy.

00:04:20.031 --> 00:04:22.420
(placid music)
In some cases,

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:24.270
the battle over App Store fees

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:26.390
has also spilled over into accusations

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:29.770
of Apple allegedly stifling competition.

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:32.690
Enter Spotify's fight with Apple.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:36.820
In 2019, Spotify filed a
formal complaint to the EU,

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.420
accusing Apple of making
it difficult for rivals

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:42.690
of Apple Music to promote
their subscription services

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.793
without using Apple's payment system.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:47.960
Apple takes a 30% cut on subscriptions

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.960
for the first year and
15% the second year.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.540
- [Announcer] If you were to
upgrade to premium in our app

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:54.720
through the App Store,

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.620
we'd have to give Apple a whopping 30%

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:59.360
of your subscription fee.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.630
- [Jaden] Spotify didn't
want to pay that fee

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.870
on its premium subscription revenue.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:07.340
So, it removed the option to
sign up from within the app.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.830
Users can still register
on Spotify's website,

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:13.060
but Apple's rules don't
allow Spotify to direct users

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:15.390
from the app to its site.

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.950
Netflix has taken a similar approach,

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.770
telling users you can't sign
up for Netflix in the app,

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.860
and Tinder charges members
more to sign up in the app

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:25.333
compared to its website.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:27.780
Apple said that developers are free

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.540
to promote other pricing
outside of the App Store,

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.630
including on television and billboards.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.590
- Certainly, when you see
the big headway companies

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:36.720
like Netflix and Spotify make that move,

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.220
you can imagine that other companies

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:41.130
are considering the same thing,

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.430
but if you'll look at a
Netflix and a Spotify,

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.300
they're in a better position
in the market themselves.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.100
So, they could potentially
do say a TV commercial

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.880
that says sign up here.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:52.530
Smaller companies may
not have that ability.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:53.560
- [Jaden] Apple has said it doesn't

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:55.490
give its own products an advantage

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.770
and that its 30% fee is in line

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.540
with the rest of the industry.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.610
But if you're an app developer,

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:05.260
your business largely depends
on just two companies,

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.920
Apple and Google.
(placid music)

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.640
Roughly 95% of all mobile
app spending in the U.S.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.050
happens on the App Store in Google Play.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:16.110
- We have Congress, the
Department of Justice,

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.660
the European Union, the FTC,

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.932
they're all investigating Apple

00:06:20.932 --> 00:06:24.550
and other technology giants
too on antitrust grounds.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.180
Google and Amazon and Facebook,

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.050
they're under the spotlight as well.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.180
The tension though with Apple

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.070
by these government entities,

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:35.710
though, has so far centered
largely around the App Store

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.840
and the App Store has become,

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:39.840
essentially, Apple's bread and butter.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.220
- [Jaden] In late September,

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.160
Apple said it would exempt some apps

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.300
from paying commissions on virtual events

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.310
hosted during the pandemic.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:49.820
But many app makers say they want Apple

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:52.530
to review its commission
policies altogether.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:56.290
And Apple's legal battles
aren't going away anytime soon.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:58.100
Depending on how things play out,

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.910
the "Fortnite" case could
have a lasting impact

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:03.410
on how much revenue
developers are able to keep

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:05.963
and how apps accept payments altogether.

00:07:05.963 --> 00:07:09.670
- The biggest thing that I'm
watching on the sidelines

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:13.300
is is Apple going to continue to take 30%?

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.920
Because that is just a
large number off the top.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.840
Which means I have that
much less to hire sales

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.220
and marketing teams,
hire other developers,

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.110
and get my product out to other people.

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:24.290
And it makes it that much harder

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:25.850
as a small business to survive.

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:27.320
- There are all sorts of possibilities

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:31.260
as to what could happen or
things could stay the status quo.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.330
But given the importance of the App Store

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:35.430
to Apple's bottom line,

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:38.090
should the outcome be such changes,

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:41.623
that could have a serious
effect on Apple's finances.

